  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical calibration and route choice modeling are demonstrated using data from a single region, the Greater Copenhagen Area, which may limit immediate generalizability to cities with vastly different cycling cultures or topographies.",
      "what": "A physics-based energy model was developed to calculate expected energy expenditure in Joules and energy intensity in Joules per metre for individual bicycle network links, accounting for slope, cruising speeds, and intersection stopping probabilities. The model was calibrated using a large-scale GPS dataset from the Greater Copenhagen Area and integrated into Multinomial Logit, Latent Class, and Mixed Logit route choice models. The analysis revealed that cyclists have a statistically significant dispreference for high-energy routes, with a distinct subgroup willing to take major detours to avoid high-intensity links.",
      "why": "It replaces proxy measures of cycling effort with a physically grounded, individual-specific metric of energy expenditure. This allows planners to evaluate how the combined effects of topography and intersection stop-and-go dynamics directly influence cyclist route choices."
    }
  },
